WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.974 --> 00:00:14.870
IAN ROWE: Hello.

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:16.630
It is an honor for
me to be speaking

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:19.960
to you as part of this
very important symposium--

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:23.740
Race in America-- History
and Controversies.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:26.350
My focus will be
on a key lever that

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:28.360
has been part of a
critical foundation

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:31.660
for empowering the
Black community to move

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:34.810
from persecution to prosperity.

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:39.670
That lever is education and
the power of school choice.

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:41.380
Throughout the
struggle for equality

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:45.190
of access and opportunity,
the ability for a Black parent

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:49.510
to choose a high-quality
school for their child

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.320
has been the great equalizer
for the Black community.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.950
But before I share a little
bit about the arduous journey

00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:59.590
of the African-American
experience of education

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:01.930
in the United States,
I'd like to tell you

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:05.080
a little bit about
my personal story.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:08.020
In the late 1960s, my
parents, brother, and I

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:10.720
emigrated from
Jamaica West Indies,

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.040
ultimately moving to Laurelton
Queens in New York, at the time

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.870
a predominantly white
middle-class community.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:22.120
Laurelton was transitioning
from a Jewish enclave

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.640
to what is now a
neighborhood mostly inhabited

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:27.850
by African-American and
Caribbean residents.

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:33.460
By 1977, I was attending IS231,
a junior high school that

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.070
had been historically
mostly white but,

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:38.860
like the surrounding
area, was rapidly

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:41.200
becoming racially integrated.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.050
Racial tensions were
growing with increasingly

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:46.210
bitter and daily disputes.

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:46.720
Driving?

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:50.230
The school leadership
to seek out solutions.

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:53.230
The local school board,
citing overcrowding

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.690
as their rationale,
voted to open

00:01:55.690 --> 00:02:00.400
an annex, a separate academic
building two miles away

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:05.290
in Rosedale, a small Irish
and Italian-American community

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:09.100
nestled on the border of
Nassau County, Long Island.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:12.910
That decision led almost
every white family

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:16.900
in my junior high school
to transfer their children

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:20.380
to the annex in
September of 1977,

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:26.890
leaving IS231 a segregated,
virtually all-Black school.

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:30.160
My Jamaican immigrant
parents, determined

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:32.410
that their two sons
would get a real shot

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:36.430
at the American dream, were
ready to transfer me as well.

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:40.510
Wherever go the White kids, so
goes the quality of education

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:41.950
was their theory.

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:45.790
I was 12 years old, and
my parents knew best.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.980
But something was
not right here.

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:50.100
I didn't want to
leave my school,

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:53.110
and I did not like the reasons
my parents were giving me

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:54.790
for doing so.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.610
It is a strange and
mildly terrifying thing

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:01.180
to consider standing up
to the people you love.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:02.830
And up until that
point in my life,

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:04.420
I had never
questioned my parents

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:06.610
about anything that mattered.

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:10.090
Why would I question the two
people who had repeatedly

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:14.500
demonstrated that they had only
my best interests at heart?

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:16.900
But something inside me said no.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:19.900
I found a voice that
I never knew existed.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:21.670
On the Sunday
evening before they

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:24.070
had to file the
transfer documents,

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:27.970
I stood in our living room,
pleading with my dad ensconced

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:30.520
in his brown recliner
and locking eyes

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:34.540
with my mom witnessing her
youngest son muster courage

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:36.940
that she had not ever seen.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.550
I begged my parents
to reconsider,

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:42.100
to let me stay in IS231.

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:43.900
I remember thinking
to myself and then

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:47.710
bravely saying out loud,
why should the other school

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:48.730
be better?

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:52.600
Why should the education
suddenly be worse in my school

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:55.840
just because most of the
kids who are going to be left

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.550
are Black?

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:01.060
I liked my teachers and I didn't
see why my education would

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:03.520
have to suffer just
because I wasn't

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:07.660
going to go to the school
with all the white kids.

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:10.660
I told my parents if they
let me stay in IS231,

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:13.120
I would work even harder.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.700
They would not regret
their decision.

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:18.690
I promised.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:23.160
They could believe in me
because I believed in myself.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:26.130
And regardless of what
they were to decide,

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:28.860
something had awakened
in me to give me

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:32.670
the confidence to respectfully
challenge my parents.

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:36.840
I was now an active player
in my own education,

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:40.860
and to a larger
degree, my own destiny.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:42.630
I made a choice.

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:47.970
I was becoming an
agent of my own uplift.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:50.550
And then something
extraordinary happened.

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:52.230
My parents relented.

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:55.280
They let me stay at IS231.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:58.290
And with the support of
my parents and teachers,

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.030
I received a public
education that

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:02.970
made me believe
anything was possible.

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:05.250
I ultimately entered Brooklyn
Technical High School,

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:07.920
went on to graduate with
a Bachelor of Science

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:10.950
in Computer Science Engineering
at Cornell University,

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:14.220
and later earned my MBA from
Harvard Business School.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:17.700
My parents and that
choice of education

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:19.750
made all the difference.

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:23.260
And now, come full-circle,
for the last decade,

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:26.580
I was the CEO of a nonprofit
network of public charter

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:30.210
schools in the heart of the
South Bronx in New York City,

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:34.050
hoping to inspire thousands
of low-income, primarily

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:37.950
Black and Hispanic students
to understand the pathways

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:42.930
through which they could become
agents of their own uplift.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:46.290
In a sense, the parents of
the Black and brown children

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:49.890
I now educated have
the same aspirations

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:53.760
that my parents had for me,
for many Black children,

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:55.890
and for children of all races.

00:05:55.890 --> 00:06:01.730
That education is the ticket
to reach the American dream.

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:04.320
But it has not always been so.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:08.250
Due to the legacy of
slavery, in the mid-1800s,

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:10.470
Black people had
very little access

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:12.810
to high-quality education.

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:15.870
In slave states,
anti-literacy laws

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:19.710
banned Black people
from accessing education

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:25.320
on the premise that a tipping
point of literate slaves

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:29.700
might lead to insurrection
and rebellion.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:32.520
For example, South
Carolina had a law

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:35.550
that would jail a
person for six months

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:41.130
and impose crippling fines if
you even attempted to teach

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:44.040
a slave to read and write.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:48.000
Things did not get much better
during the Jim Crow era, which

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:51.350
legally segregated schools.

00:06:51.350 --> 00:06:54.290
During that time, like
much of everyday life,

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:57.650
public schools were
segregated by race.

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:01.640
They were separate and
profoundly unequal.

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:04.490
There were huge disparities
in funding between Black

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:08.780
and white schools, facilities,
access to equipment,

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:12.500
and most importantly, the
quality of the education

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:14.000
itself.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:17.510
In 1896, the Supreme
Court in the case

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:22.340
of Plessy versus Ferguson
ruled that separate but equal

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:25.520
was fair and it
was not a violation

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:30.350
of the 14th Amendment, requiring
equal protection to all.

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:33.560
This ruling meant that the
federal government would not

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:38.420
challenge segregation, even
despite the deeply inferior

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:41.720
conditions that Black
students were subject to,

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:43.970
especially in education.

00:07:43.970 --> 00:07:49.010
Separate but equal and Jim
Crow remained unchallenged

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:52.600
until Brown versus Board
of Education in 1954

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:56.300
and the Civil
Rights Act of 1964.

00:07:56.300 --> 00:08:00.320
It is against this backdrop,
when racial segregation was

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:05.120
at its worst, that an unlikely
partnership transformed

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:08.750
education for two
generations of Black students

00:08:08.750 --> 00:08:11.030
in the rural South.

00:08:11.030 --> 00:08:14.120
Educator, activist,
public intellectual,

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:16.070
and founder of the
Tuskegee Institute.

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:17.550
Booker T. Washington.

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:18.050
Had?

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:22.550
Himself experience
firsthand the destitution

00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:26.690
and profound racial
disparities in the rural South.

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:29.240
Despite these
obstacles, Washington's

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:33.350
perseverance and disciplined
upbringing eventually

00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:35.240
led to an education
at what is now

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:37.760
known as Hampton University.

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:42.049
And as an adult, Washington
dreamed of a school building

00:08:42.049 --> 00:08:45.560
project for Black communities
throughout the South

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:50.000
that could help begin to lift
Black people out of poverty.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:52.850
Washington presented
his ambitious idea

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:56.990
to build a network of schools
to Julius Rosenwald, who

00:08:56.990 --> 00:09:00.440
at the time was a CEO
of the nation's largest

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:02.390
retailer, Sears.

00:09:02.390 --> 00:09:05.450
Rosenwald was a supporter
of the Tuskegee Institute,

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:09.710
so he had a deep respectful
relationship with Washington.

00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:12.440
Washington collaborated
with Julius Rosenwald,

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:15.140
and together they
crafted a plan to make

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:18.620
Washington's dream a reality.

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:22.070
In a region of the United
States in which Jim Crow

00:09:22.070 --> 00:09:25.610
racial segregation
was law, lynching

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:28.340
was a common and
a brutal reality,

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:32.240
and rural poverty was widespread
on both sides of the color

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:36.080
line, these Rosenwald
facilities were typically

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:39.470
constructed, maintained,
and partially

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:43.160
funded by the populations
that they served,

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:46.490
becoming centers of
local social life

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:49.870
and sources of community pride.

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:52.480
Over the years to
construct the buildings,

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:55.270
Rosenwald contributed
$4.4 million,

00:09:55.270 --> 00:09:58.870
or what today would
be nearly $80 million.

00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:03.760
And importantly,
$4.7 million came

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:09.100
from local Black communities in
the form of cash, labor, land,

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:10.870
and materials.

00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:14.440
Washington and Rosenwald had
launched a challenge grant

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:19.540
program that ultimately led to
the unprecedented construction

00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:29.050
of more than 5,000 school
buildings, 4,977 schools, 217

00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:35.800
teachers' homes, and 163
shop buildings in 15 states

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:40.600
from Maryland to Texas, all
dedicated to the education

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:42.280
of Black students.

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:46.090
While the required donation from
African-American communities

00:10:46.090 --> 00:10:50.290
amounted to double taxation,
their leaders nonetheless

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:54.730
called on residents to organize
fundraising drives to cover

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:57.910
their share of each new school.

00:10:57.910 --> 00:11:02.340
"Get busy, Laurel, or
hang your heads in shame,"

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:06.700
admonished the Prince George's
Colored Public School Trustees

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:10.840
in a 1924 report describing
the existing pitiful

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:12.940
buildings in the town of Laurel.

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:17.140
These efforts to build or
renovate new school buildings

00:11:17.140 --> 00:11:18.580
were impressive.

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:22.780
In one instance, struggling
sharecroppers set aside

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:25.840
an area planted with
cotton as the quote,

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:29.980
"Rosenwald patch," end quote,
and donated the profits

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:32.890
from its sale to the school.

00:11:32.890 --> 00:11:36.120
Children saved pennies
in snuff boxes.

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:39.760
And at one fundraising
rally, an old man

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:45.910
who had been a slave offered
his life savings of $38 to,

00:11:45.910 --> 00:11:52.200
quote, "see the children of my
grandchildren have a chance,"

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:54.160
end quote.

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:57.850
In the years between World
War I and World War II,

00:11:57.850 --> 00:12:00.670
the Rosenwald Schools
helped close the gap

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:04.030
between White and Black
academic achievement.

00:12:04.030 --> 00:12:06.970
Researchers at the
Chicago Federal Reserve

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:10.300
concluded that in the regions
where the Rosenwald Rural

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:15.010
Schools Initiative built
most of its 5,000 schools,

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:17.740
the education gap between
Southern-born Black

00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:20.860
and white males narrowed
sharply in areas

00:12:20.860 --> 00:12:24.670
such as school attendance,
literacy, and cognitive test

00:12:24.670 --> 00:12:26.080
scores.

00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:29.740
Using census data and
World War II records,

00:12:29.740 --> 00:12:33.430
the researchers found
that Rosenwald's program

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:37.600
explained a stunning
40% narrowing

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:40.740
of the racial education gap.

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:43.710
While the Rosenwald
schools were thriving,

00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:48.960
a major case was being litigated
over segregation in 1954.

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:52.140
The Supreme Court
made a landmark ruling

00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:56.250
that school segregation was
unconstitutional in Brown

00:12:56.250 --> 00:12:58.500
versus Board of Education.

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:00.690
The fundamental premise
was that, quote,

00:13:00.690 --> 00:13:07.370
"Separate is inherently
unequal," end quote.

00:13:07.370 --> 00:13:13.810
So exclusively Black or white
schools were now outlawed.

00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:16.620
So a decision
intended to improve

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:19.440
outcomes for Black
children assumed

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:23.220
separate had to mean inferior.

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:26.640
The terrible irony is
that decision actually

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:29.490
had the perverse
impact of making

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:33.000
the very existence of
the Rosenwald Schools

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:35.350
a violation of the law.

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:39.390
So even with its extraordinary
record of success,

00:13:39.390 --> 00:13:43.020
the Rosenwald schools
were quickly abandoned.

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:47.010
Despite student protests over
their closing and demolition,

00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:51.070
most of the schools
were gone by the 1970s.

00:13:51.070 --> 00:13:53.010
The Rosenwald Schools
remain today one

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:56.310
of the greatest examples
of strong partnership

00:13:56.310 --> 00:14:01.320
across racial lines, Black
excellence, and ironically,

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:04.830
the victim of
unintended consequences.

00:14:04.830 --> 00:14:06.990
Fast forward to today.

00:14:06.990 --> 00:14:09.570
While progress has been
made with more than 3

00:14:09.570 --> 00:14:13.260
million Black students
enrolled in college today,

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:16.380
there are still deep
academic racial disparities

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:18.720
in pockets of American society.

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:21.900
For example, of the
nearly 2,000 public school

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:24.660
students beginning high
school in the South Bronx,

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:30.120
where I led a network of
charter schools in 2015, only 2%

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:33.330
of kids in this
district graduated ready

00:14:33.330 --> 00:14:35.880
for college four years later.

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:39.180
A shocking 98% of
students either

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:41.790
dropped out of high
school before completing

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:46.080
their senior year or they
did manage to graduate,

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:48.000
but would still be
required to take

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:51.150
remedial classes in
community college

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:55.290
due to low math and reading
scores on state exams.

00:14:55.290 --> 00:14:59.100
By contrast, charter public
schools in the South Bronx

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:01.950
continue to outperform
their district peers

00:15:01.950 --> 00:15:06.060
in both math and reading in
third through eighth grade

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:08.760
by a substantial margin.

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:11.100
It comes as no
surprise that parents

00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:14.910
in these predominantly Black
and Hispanic communities

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:18.570
encompassing two of the
poorest congressional districts

00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:23.640
in the country are desperate
for higher-quality education

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:24.780
options.

00:15:24.780 --> 00:15:26.760
The New York City
Charter Center reports

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:32.010
that in the Bronx alone,
more than 25,000 families

00:15:32.010 --> 00:15:36.180
applied for just over 9,000
available seats in Bronx

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:37.950
charter schools.

00:15:37.950 --> 00:15:41.070
This overwhelming
demand demonstrates

00:15:41.070 --> 00:15:44.130
that parents want the
best for their children

00:15:44.130 --> 00:15:47.310
and should be given
the power of choice.

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:50.550
Many of these parents may have
faced racial discrimination

00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:54.230
and other challenges
in their own lives,

00:15:54.230 --> 00:15:57.390
and fear that their
children might as well.

00:15:57.390 --> 00:16:00.680
But they know that
a great education

00:16:00.680 --> 00:16:03.770
can make a huge difference.

00:16:03.770 --> 00:16:05.630
They do not believe
that their children are

00:16:05.630 --> 00:16:09.380
doomed to be shackled by
the horrors of America's

00:16:09.380 --> 00:16:11.430
legacy of slavery.

00:16:11.430 --> 00:16:16.220
They know that if teachers had
the highest of expectations

00:16:16.220 --> 00:16:19.190
and were empowered
with a curriculum that

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:21.440
could help their
children develop

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:26.900
character-based strengths
like integrity, resiliency,

00:16:26.900 --> 00:16:29.510
and agency, the
belief that you can

00:16:29.510 --> 00:16:32.060
be the master of
your own destiny,

00:16:32.060 --> 00:16:36.440
then those children could
lead life on their own terms,

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:39.810
even in the face of
structural barriers.

00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:42.510
That is why I must
mention the 1619

00:16:42.510 --> 00:16:46.350
Project, a major initiative
and series of essays

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:48.720
from the New York
Times Magazine.

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:51.330
For a year, the authors
have repeated the claim

00:16:51.330 --> 00:16:55.260
that the country's, quote,
"true founding," end quote,

00:16:55.260 --> 00:17:01.350
was 1619 and not 1776, when
the Declaration of Independence

00:17:01.350 --> 00:17:03.110
was signed.

00:17:03.110 --> 00:17:08.310
1619 claims that America was
founded as a slavocracy, not

00:17:08.310 --> 00:17:12.000
a democracy, that
anti-Black racism

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:15.670
runs in the very
DNA of the country.

00:17:15.670 --> 00:17:20.589
The 1619 Project also claims
that America's founding ideals

00:17:20.589 --> 00:17:22.869
were false when
they were written,

00:17:22.869 --> 00:17:26.319
and that the nation
was founded on a lie.

00:17:26.319 --> 00:17:30.730
Despite the fact that the 1619
Project has been discredited

00:17:30.730 --> 00:17:34.420
by dozens of the most
well-respected historians

00:17:34.420 --> 00:17:37.600
in the country,
particularly the claim

00:17:37.600 --> 00:17:42.490
that the Civil War was
fought to defend slavery,

00:17:42.490 --> 00:17:44.590
the New York Times
was not satisfied

00:17:44.590 --> 00:17:47.560
with just the special
edition of the magazine.

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:49.900
They partnered with
the Pulitzer Center

00:17:49.900 --> 00:17:52.750
to create a curriculum
that has now been adopted

00:17:52.750 --> 00:17:55.060
in thousands of classrooms.

00:17:55.060 --> 00:17:59.230
School districts in Chicago,
Illinois, Washington, DC,

00:17:59.230 --> 00:18:02.620
and Buffalo, New York began
implementing the project

00:18:02.620 --> 00:18:06.070
in their K-12 course materials.

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:10.000
These are some of the lowest
academically performing

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:13.980
schools in the country.

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:17.010
As an educator who has spent
the last 10 years working

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:20.490
with families who believe
and want their child

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:23.910
to have the American
dream, the last thing

00:18:23.910 --> 00:18:27.330
their children need to
hear is false information

00:18:27.330 --> 00:18:28.980
about our country.

00:18:28.980 --> 00:18:31.050
Yes, America has
a terrible history

00:18:31.050 --> 00:18:35.520
of slavery and oppression, but
America is not defined solely

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:37.950
by its legacy of slavery.

00:18:37.950 --> 00:18:42.720
We want all children to know
of the legacy of excellence

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:46.620
alongside the legacy of
slavery, and the lessons

00:18:46.620 --> 00:18:50.380
that are relevant to
All-American children.

00:18:50.380 --> 00:18:55.240
There are innumerable examples
of individuals and groups

00:18:55.240 --> 00:18:59.500
that have gone from
persecution to prosperity

00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:04.000
by embracing America's
founding ideals of family,

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:09.280
free enterprise, faith, hard
work, and entrepreneurship.

00:19:09.280 --> 00:19:13.000
That is why I'm so proud to
say that in the same spirit

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:17.680
that Booker T. Washington
demonstrated to envision how

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:21.400
education could be a
pathway to prosperity,

00:19:21.400 --> 00:19:25.810
how he had an idea to
build a network of schools

00:19:25.810 --> 00:19:29.770
throughout the South, even in
the face of bitter and often

00:19:29.770 --> 00:19:33.070
lethal racial
discrimination, well,

00:19:33.070 --> 00:19:37.540
today, a group of talented
Black scholars and activists

00:19:37.540 --> 00:19:40.610
have come together
to do the same.

00:19:40.610 --> 00:19:46.300
This initiative, called 1776
Unites, is led by Bob Woodson.

00:19:46.300 --> 00:19:50.110
For 40 years, Bob
Woodson developed a list

00:19:50.110 --> 00:19:54.010
of 10 principles that
he has used in order

00:19:54.010 --> 00:19:57.550
to help local
communities transform

00:19:57.550 --> 00:20:02.780
struggling neighborhoods and to
empower the people within them.

00:20:02.780 --> 00:20:09.510
These 10 principles include
competence, integrity,

00:20:09.510 --> 00:20:16.340
transparency, resilience,
witness, innovation,

00:20:16.340 --> 00:20:23.510
inspiration, agency,
access, and grace.

00:20:23.510 --> 00:20:30.410
These 10 principles now
undergird a new 1776 Unites

00:20:30.410 --> 00:20:32.810
curriculum which
we'll ultimately

00:20:32.810 --> 00:20:36.890
develop into a K
program for all schools

00:20:36.890 --> 00:20:41.510
wherever character formation
is happening for children.

00:20:41.510 --> 00:20:45.200
Every lesson will connect
back to one or more

00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:47.830
of the Woodson principles.

00:20:47.830 --> 00:20:53.140
The 1776 Unites curriculum
offers authentic, inspiring

00:20:53.140 --> 00:20:56.710
stories from America's
past and present

00:20:56.710 --> 00:21:00.400
that show what is best
in our national character

00:21:00.400 --> 00:21:05.020
and what our freedom makes
possible, even in the most

00:21:05.020 --> 00:21:07.360
difficult circumstances.

00:21:07.360 --> 00:21:14.440
1776 Unites includes two
major components, a look back

00:21:14.440 --> 00:21:16.260
and a look forward.

00:21:16.260 --> 00:21:19.330
The look back maintains
a special focus

00:21:19.330 --> 00:21:24.490
on stories and lesson plans
that celebrate Black excellence,

00:21:24.490 --> 00:21:29.800
reject victimhood culture, and
showcase African-Americans past

00:21:29.800 --> 00:21:34.660
and present who have
prospered by embracing

00:21:34.660 --> 00:21:39.400
America's founding ideals
of free enterprise, family,

00:21:39.400 --> 00:21:43.880
hard work,
entrepreneurship, and faith.

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:48.140
Two examples include Biddy
Mason, an amazing woman who

00:21:48.140 --> 00:21:51.260
was born a slave but
died a millionaire

00:21:51.260 --> 00:21:56.360
and a philanthropist, and Elijah
McCoy, a son of escaped slaves

00:21:56.360 --> 00:22:00.680
who became a talented engineer
and inventor whose products

00:22:00.680 --> 00:22:05.810
were so superior that inferior
knockoffs were created

00:22:05.810 --> 00:22:10.400
to the point that when engineers
went to buy McCoy's products

00:22:10.400 --> 00:22:14.120
and were offered the
copies, they said, no, I

00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:17.560
want the real McCoy.

00:22:17.560 --> 00:22:19.570
Many Americans may
know that phrase

00:22:19.570 --> 00:22:23.560
but do not know it is
connected to a Black engineer,

00:22:23.560 --> 00:22:25.360
the son of slaves.

00:22:25.360 --> 00:22:28.240
These are just two of
the multitude of stories

00:22:28.240 --> 00:22:33.700
that will be in the look back
section of the 1776 Unites

00:22:33.700 --> 00:22:35.240
curriculum.

00:22:35.240 --> 00:22:38.230
The look forward
component includes

00:22:38.230 --> 00:22:40.960
units that teach
life lessons that

00:22:40.960 --> 00:22:45.820
help the next generation
understand the series of life

00:22:45.820 --> 00:22:48.280
decisions that give
them the greatest

00:22:48.280 --> 00:22:52.270
likelihood of economic
success as they make choices

00:22:52.270 --> 00:22:55.540
as they make their passage
into young adulthood.

00:22:55.540 --> 00:22:58.580
Despite real racial
barriers that are out there,

00:22:58.580 --> 00:23:01.960
there are millions of
examples of Black excellence.

00:23:01.960 --> 00:23:04.510
We constantly study poverty.

00:23:04.510 --> 00:23:06.970
What can we learn from
Black Americans who've

00:23:06.970 --> 00:23:10.660
been successful in
pursuing the American dream

00:23:10.660 --> 00:23:14.530
and their decisions around
education, work, marriage,

00:23:14.530 --> 00:23:16.000
and children?

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:18.370
For example, teaching
the data associated

00:23:18.370 --> 00:23:21.640
with what's called the success
sequence, the data that

00:23:21.640 --> 00:23:24.430
shows if you finish
a high school degree,

00:23:24.430 --> 00:23:28.840
secure a full-time job of any
kind, so you learn the dignity

00:23:28.840 --> 00:23:33.340
and discipline of work, and
if you have children, marriage

00:23:33.340 --> 00:23:38.800
first, 98% of the people
who follow those steps avoid

00:23:38.800 --> 00:23:41.680
poverty, and the
vast majority end up

00:23:41.680 --> 00:23:43.930
in the middle class or beyond.

00:23:43.930 --> 00:23:47.140
That's the kind of information
that young people of all races

00:23:47.140 --> 00:23:50.170
need to know to increase
their likelihood of being

00:23:50.170 --> 00:23:52.570
economically successful.

00:23:52.570 --> 00:23:55.150
In the case of my
own parents, that

00:23:55.150 --> 00:23:57.610
is how they achieved
the American dream

00:23:57.610 --> 00:24:01.810
and made it possible
for me to do the same.

00:24:01.810 --> 00:24:04.810
I often wonder what would have
happened if my parents had not

00:24:04.810 --> 00:24:07.780
acquiesced, and
instead insisted that I

00:24:07.780 --> 00:24:12.070
attend the annex with
almost all White students.

00:24:12.070 --> 00:24:14.980
Would I have exhibited the
same level of ownership

00:24:14.980 --> 00:24:19.420
over my learning as I did when
I convinced them to grant me

00:24:19.420 --> 00:24:22.190
my wish to stay?

00:24:22.190 --> 00:24:24.980
Yet in allowing me
to forge a path that

00:24:24.980 --> 00:24:27.350
went against their
better judgment,

00:24:27.350 --> 00:24:30.290
my parents taught me
a valuable lesson.

00:24:30.290 --> 00:24:34.970
My effort could and would
have to influence my outcomes.

00:24:34.970 --> 00:24:39.290
At the time, I felt an enormous
added responsibility to make

00:24:39.290 --> 00:24:43.310
my parents' decision worth
the trust that they had placed

00:24:43.310 --> 00:24:44.460
in me.

00:24:44.460 --> 00:24:48.890
I am convinced the feeling that
I had on that long ago Sunday

00:24:48.890 --> 00:24:53.090
evening, the faith I
was expressing in myself

00:24:53.090 --> 00:24:57.500
and appealing to my parents
to reluctantly accept

00:24:57.500 --> 00:25:03.140
was what I now understand to
be my sense of personal agency.

00:25:03.140 --> 00:25:05.420
That self-belief
was the precursor

00:25:05.420 --> 00:25:09.260
to the ensuing efforts and
actions necessary for me

00:25:09.260 --> 00:25:13.340
to fulfill the commitment
that I had made to my parents,

00:25:13.340 --> 00:25:16.380
and most importantly to myself.

00:25:16.380 --> 00:25:18.620
But what was also
important was the fact

00:25:18.620 --> 00:25:23.180
that my parents had the
ability to choose and to chart

00:25:23.180 --> 00:25:28.130
a path through education
to make life better for me,

00:25:28.130 --> 00:25:30.920
and, by extension,
the next generation.

00:25:30.920 --> 00:25:33.980
It is through this type of
innovation and commitment,

00:25:33.980 --> 00:25:36.530
whether it be Booker T.
Washington, or Bob Woodson,

00:25:36.530 --> 00:25:40.430
or my parents, that young
Americans of all races

00:25:40.430 --> 00:25:43.520
can understand that
even while race

00:25:43.520 --> 00:25:46.370
has been an issue
in our country,

00:25:46.370 --> 00:25:49.790
it is actually the
power of education,

00:25:49.790 --> 00:25:53.000
the power of America's
founding ideals

00:25:53.000 --> 00:25:55.730
and our founding
documents signed

00:25:55.730 --> 00:26:00.890
in 1776 that have
within them tools

00:26:00.890 --> 00:26:04.760
for self-renewal
and self-betterment.

00:26:04.760 --> 00:26:08.030
In chapter 13 of de
Tocqueville's America,

00:26:08.030 --> 00:26:12.140
he said, quote, "The
greatness of America

00:26:12.140 --> 00:26:14.900
lies not in being
more enlightened

00:26:14.900 --> 00:26:17.510
than any other
nation, but rather

00:26:17.510 --> 00:26:22.130
in her ability to repair
her faults," end quote.

00:26:22.130 --> 00:26:25.280
I have always found that
statement compelling,

00:26:25.280 --> 00:26:27.170
because it resonates
with the notion

00:26:27.170 --> 00:26:31.310
that America is always
in pursuit of becoming

00:26:31.310 --> 00:26:33.440
a more perfect union.

00:26:33.440 --> 00:26:37.550
While the founders laid
out, inspiring ideals,

00:26:37.550 --> 00:26:42.090
we are in a constant effort
to fully live up to them.

00:26:42.090 --> 00:26:46.370
That is the critical story
about race and education,

00:26:46.370 --> 00:26:49.640
because how we make
steady progress is

00:26:49.640 --> 00:26:54.620
through reminding and embracing
those founding ideals,

00:26:54.620 --> 00:26:57.170
helping our next
generation to do that,

00:26:57.170 --> 00:26:59.990
and not rejecting them.

00:26:59.990 --> 00:27:01.840
Thank you.

